          "lesson": {
            "prerequisite_note": "Builds on linear LVM creation and reports. This lesson teaches deliberate retirement of supplied unused raw allocations. Filesystem-bearing data, real activation/mount checks, force and secure erasure are outside the modeled subset.",
            "concepts": [
              {
                "title": "Remove the intended allocation, preserve the data",
                "text": "A logical volume reserves pieces of its group. Removing an unused raw LV returns those extents to that same pool; it does not shrink the physical device or increase a neighboring filesystem. This exercise identifies the raw allocations that are no longer needed. The ledger and other data volumes must stay unchanged."
              },
              {
                "title": "Inspect before making a destructive change",
                "text": "Read lvs and the supplied inventory before choosing a name. Here lvremove -y VG/LV explicitly confirms removal of one raw LV; /dev/VG/LV and --yes are supported alternatives. Real lvremove can destroy data. Our refusal of filesystem-bearing removal is a simulation boundary, not a safety promise about Linux."
              },
              {
                "title": "Verify the current result, not a success message",
                "text": "Use lvs to confirm the intended names are absent, and vgs to see the returned free extents. Every unrelated LV, group, file and device must remain. Check work evaluates that state; inspection output alone does not complete the task."
              }
            ],
            "description": "Workshop has ten 4 MiB extents. Ledger uses two, trial one, and buffer two; five are free. Retiring trial and buffer returns three extents, so eight become free. Ledger and its files remain exactly as supplied.",
