            "prerequisite_note": "Builds on linear LVM creation and reports. This lesson teaches deliberate retirement of supplied unused raw allocations. Filesystem-bearing data, real activation/mount checks, force and secure erasure are outside the modeled subset.",
            "concepts": [
              {
                "title": "Retire storage from the inside out",
                "text": "An LV belongs to a volume group; a PV supplies storage to that group. First remove only the unused raw LVs named in the plan. Then vgremove NAME can remove the empty group in this subset. A nonempty group is refused here; real vgremove can remove contained volumes after confirmation and has broader destructive behavior."
              },
              {
                "title": "An absent group does not mean an absent PV label",
                "text": "After vgremove, its devices are still labeled physical volumes, now without a group. Read pvs: pvremove /dev/NAME... clears only the specified unowned PV labels. Do not clear an unrelated orphan PV merely because it has no group. No force, vgreduce or filesystem-bearing removal is modeled."
              },
              {
                "title": "No label is not secure erasure",
                "text": "Clearing an LVM label stops that device being recognized as a PV. Its size does not change, and this does not format a filesystem, wipe every byte or securely erase data. The inventory reports no modeled PV label; it makes no claim about recoverable media contents."
              },
              {
                "title": "Inspect before making a destructive change",
                "text": "Read lvs and the supplied inventory before choosing a name. Here lvremove -y VG/LV explicitly confirms removal of one raw LV; /dev/VG/LV and --yes are supported alternatives. Real lvremove can destroy data. Our refusal of filesystem-bearing removal is a simulation boundary, not a safety promise about Linux."
              },
              {
                "title": "Verify all three layers",
                "text": "lvs and vgs confirm retired names are absent. pvs and the supplied device facts confirm the intended labels are gone while unrelated PVs and protected data remain. An empty lvs report by itself is insufficient. This exercise declares the raw allocations unused; real activation, mount and process-use checks are outside this subset."
              }
